@page "/themes"

<SeoHead Title="Make Quark match your application | Quark Suite" Description="Choose shadcn-compatible theme tokens or generate a shared C# theme. Learn which CSS files to load and how to switch light and dark mode." />

<DocumentationPage Title="Make Quark match your application" Description="Choose shadcn-compatible theme tokens or generate a shared C# theme. Learn which CSS files to load and how to switch light and dark mode.">

<Section Id="choose-a-source">
    <H2>Choose who owns the theme tokens</H2>
    <Paragraph>Start with the Tailwind generator's shadcn-compatible defaults. Use a JSON configuration when you want a palette and token overrides. Use a C# <Code>Theme</Code> when your application needs a shared, typed definition of tokens and component styling.</Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>These are two ways to author the token input. A separate runtime theme stylesheet can also contain component rules, so it has a different job from the Tailwind token file.</Paragraph>
</Section>

<Section Id="json-theme">
    <H2>Option 1: configure tokens in JSON</H2>
    <Paragraph>The enabled Tailwind build can create <Code>tailwind/quark-shadcn.theme.json</Code>. Choose a base palette, theme, chart palette, and radius:</Paragraph>
    <CodeEditor Text="@themeJson" Language="json" ReadOnly="true" AutoHeight="true" Minimap="false" ShowCopyButton="true" />
    <Paragraph>Explicitly select that configuration in the app project when JSON should own the tokens:</Paragraph>
    <CodeEditor Text="@themeConfig" Language="xml" ReadOnly="true" AutoHeight="true" Minimap="false" ShowCopyButton="true" />
    <Paragraph>This matters when <Code>tailwind/quark-theme.generated.css</Code> already exists. Without explicit shadcn configuration, Tailwind reuses that generated token input. Rebuild after changing your selected theme source.</Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>You can also override individual variables with the JSON <Code>light</Code>, <Code>dark</Code>, and <Code>inline</Code> objects. <Anchor Href="https://github.com/soenneker/soenneker.quark.gen.tailwind">See the Tailwind generator configuration reference</Anchor>.</Paragraph>
</Section>

<Section Id="csharp-theme">
    <H2>Option 2: define an application theme in C#</H2>
    <CodeEditor Text="@themePackages" Language="shell" ReadOnly="true" AutoHeight="true" Minimap="false" ShowCopyButton="true" />
    <Paragraph>Create a class with exactly one public static method or property returning <Code>Theme</Code>, and annotate the class with the output path:</Paragraph>
    <CodeEditor Text="@theme" Language="csharp" ReadOnly="true" AutoHeight="true" Minimap="false" ShowCopyButton="true" />
    <Paragraph><Code>ThemeTokens</Code> lives in <Code>Soenneker.Quark.Tokens</Code>. Unchanged tokens keep their defaults. The factory runs during the build: keep it deterministic and independent of authenticated users, browser storage, or network calls.</Paragraph>
</Section>

<Section Id="outputs">
    <H2>Load the right output in the right order</H2>
    <Paragraph>The example defines both tokens and a button rule. A factory containing only tokens produces the Tailwind token input; the runner skips an empty component stylesheet. Load the separate application-theme stylesheet when your factory produces component rules.</Paragraph>
    <UnorderedList><UnorderedListItem><Code>wwwroot/css/app-theme.css</Code> and <Code>app-theme.min.css</Code>: runtime theme CSS. Load one.</UnorderedListItem><UnorderedListItem><Code>tailwind/quark-theme.generated.css</Code>: token input consumed by the Tailwind build.</UnorderedListItem><UnorderedListItem><Code>wwwroot/css/quark-tailwind.min.css</Code>: compiled utility CSS for the application.</UnorderedListItem></UnorderedList>
    <CodeEditor Text="@themeStyles" Language="html" ReadOnly="true" AutoHeight="true" Minimap="false" ShowCopyButton="true" />
    <Paragraph>Load the application theme after Tailwind. If your C# theme is the source of token values, avoid a competing explicit shadcn configuration that would replace those tokens.</Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>This is the same separation used in Leadping: a named C# theme defines light/dark tokens and component styling, and the app loads the Tailwind stylesheet before its application-theme stylesheet.</Paragraph>
</Section>

<Section Id="dark-mode">
    <H2>Switch between light and dark mode</H2>
    <Paragraph>Theme generation defines both sets of values. Runtime theme interop selects which one is active. <Code>AddQuarkSuiteAsScoped()</Code> registers <Code>IThemeInterop</Code>.</Paragraph>
    <CodeEditor Text="@themeToggle" Language="razor" ReadOnly="true" AutoHeight="true" Minimap="false" ShowCopyButton="true" />
    <Paragraph>The demo wraps this in a shared theme service so the header and other controls use the same state. Initialize browser interop after the first interactive render.</Paragraph>
</Section>

<Section Id="startup-theme">
    <H2>Apply the stored theme before the app starts</H2>
    <Paragraph>The Quark demo also initializes its theme from the document head to reduce a light/dark flash during startup:</Paragraph>
    <CodeEditor Text="@earlyTheme" Language="html" ReadOnly="true" AutoHeight="true" Minimap="false" ShowCopyButton="true" />
    <Paragraph>This selects the runtime preference; it does not compile or generate CSS. Keep the generated stylesheets in the head as well.</Paragraph>
</Section>

<Section Id="semantic-colors">
    <H2>Let the components use semantic colors</H2>
    <Paragraph>Use pairs such as <Code>BackgroundColor.Card</Code> and <Code>TextColor.CardForeground</Code>. The theme changes the values while the component markup stays the same. Hard-coded colors remain useful for specific artwork, but they do not automatically become your dark-mode palette.</Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>Continue with <Anchor Href="properties">typed styling</Anchor> or <Anchor Href="troubleshooting#theme-changes">theme troubleshooting</Anchor>.</Paragraph>
</Section>

</DocumentationPage>

@code {
    private const string themeJson = @"{
  ""baseColor"": ""Neutral"",
  ""theme"": ""Blue"",
  ""chartColor"": ""Emerald"",
  ""radius"": ""Default""
}";

    private const string themeConfig = @"<PropertyGroup>
  <ShadcnThemeConfig>tailwind/quark-shadcn.theme.json</ShadcnThemeConfig>
</PropertyGroup>";

    private const string themePackages = @"dotnet add package Soenneker.Quark.Gen.Themes";

    private const string theme = @"using Soenneker.Quark;
using Soenneker.Quark.Gen.Themes;
using Soenneker.Quark.Tokens;

[GenerateQuarkThemeCss(""wwwroot/css/app-theme.css"")]
public static class AppTheme
{
    public static Theme Build() => new()
    {
        Name = ""MyApp"",
        Tokens = new ThemeTokens
        {
            Light =
            {
                Primary = ""#2563eb"",
                PrimaryForeground = ""#ffffff""
            },
            Dark =
            {
                Primary = ""#93c5fd"",
                PrimaryForeground = ""#172554""
            }
        },
        Buttons = new ButtonOptions
        {
            Rounded = Rounded.Lg
        }
    };
}";

    private const string themeStyles = @"<link rel=""stylesheet"" href=""css/quark-tailwind.min.css"" />
<link rel=""stylesheet"" href=""css/app-theme.min.css"" />";

    private const string themeToggle = @"@using Soenneker.Quark
@inject IThemeInterop ThemeInterop

<ThemeToggleButton IsDark=""_isDark"" OnClick=""ToggleTheme"" />

@code {
    private bool _isDark;

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender)
            return;

        _isDark = await ThemeInterop.Initialize();
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    private async Task ToggleTheme()
    {
        _isDark = await ThemeInterop.Toggle();
    }
}";

    private const string earlyTheme = @"<script type=""module"">
  import { initialize } from ""./_content/Soenneker.Quark.Suite/js/themeinterop.js"";
  initialize();
</script>";
}
